<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Subline's hardened-runtime entitlements.

      READ THIS BEFORE ADDING ONE. Every entitlement here is a hole punched in
      the hardened runtime, and Apple's notary service reads the list. The rule
      applied to this file is: an entitlement is present only if a NAMED thing
      the app does stops working without it. Nothing is here "because Electron
      apps usually have it".

      ---------------------------------------------------------------------
      WHAT IS *NOT* HERE, AND THE ONE THAT MATTERS MOST
      ---------------------------------------------------------------------

      There is NO entitlement that lets an application modify another
      application's bundle. Subline's entire purpose — writing
      `Discord.app/Contents/Resources/app.asar` — is governed by TCC's
      App Management service (`kTCCServiceSystemPolicyAppBundles`), which is a
      *user grant*, made once in System Settings › Privacy & Security ›
      App Management. It cannot be requested, declared, or entitled.

      Two consequences for this file, both load-bearing:

        1. `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is DELIBERATELY ABSENT. A sandboxed
           app cannot hold App Management over an arbitrary bundle, so adding
           the sandbox would not tighten Subline — it would stop it working
           and there would be no entitlement that brought it back.

        2. The grant is keyed to the CODE-SIGNING IDENTITY, which is why
           spec §4 requires Developer ID rather than ad-hoc signing: a stable
           Team ID is what makes the permission persist across Subline's own
           updates, and what lets the LaunchAgent helper (the same bundle, run
           with `--helper`) write to Discord under the permission the user
           already granted.

      Also absent, and each for a reason:
        · `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` — Subline loads no
          third-party native code. Every dylib and framework inside the bundle
          is signed with our own identity by electron-builder.
        · `com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables` — nothing
          here sets DYLD_*; allowing it would let an attacker with local write
          access inject a library into a process that edits another app.
        · `com.apple.security.device.*`, `com.apple.security.network.server` —
          no camera, no microphone, no listening socket.
    -->

    <!--
      V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at runtime. Without this, the
      renderer aborts on launch. Required by every Electron app.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      V8 also maps writable-then-executable pages outside the JIT entitlement's
      narrow allowance. Chromium has needed this pair for as long as the
      hardened runtime has existed; dropping it produces a crash at startup,
      not a permission dialog.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      REQUIRED BY A FEATURE, not by Electron.

      Two things Subline does are Apple events:
        · `requestQuit` runs `osascript -e 'tell application "Discord" to quit'`
          — spec §7's "offer to quit it; never force-kill silently". A kill
          would lose whatever the user was typing, so the polite request is the
          product decision and this entitlement is its cost.
        · the helper's `display notification` for a failure it cannot repair
          (§6, §7).

      Under the hardened runtime, a process without this entitlement gets
      `errAEEventNotPermitted` — the script fails with no prompt at all, which
      would present as "the Quit button does nothing".

      It pairs with `NSAppleEventsUsageDescription` in Info.plist: THAT string
      is the one the user reads in the Automation prompt, and unlike App
      Management it is ours to write.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
